## Key Concepts

This video covers how to use **Angular services** to manage data and share logic across components. The primary goal is to refactor an application so that data is fetched from a central service rather than being hardcoded in components.

## Creating a Service


-   **Purpose**: A service is an injectable class used to encapsulate logic and data, making it a **single source of truth** for your application.
-   **CLI Command**: Generate a new service using the Angular CLI.
    ```bash
    ng g s housing
    ```

> [!NOTE]
> `s` is the abbreviation for `service`.

## Service Implementation


An Angular service is a TypeScript class decorated with `@Injectable()`.

-   **`@Injectable()` Decorator**: Marks a class as available to be provided and injected as a dependency.
-   **`providedIn: 'root'`**: This metadata tells Angular to provide the service at the application's root level. This makes a single, shared instance of the service available to be injected throughout the app.

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HousingLocation } from './housinglocation';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class HousingService {
  protected housingLocationList: HousingLocation[] = [/* ... data ... */];

  getAllHousingLocations(): HousingLocation[] {
    return this.housingLocationList;
  }

  getHousingLocationById(id: number): HousingLocation | undefined {
    return this.housingLocationList.find(
      (housingLocation) => housingLocation.id === id
    );
  }
}
```

## Dependency Injection (DI)


DI is a design pattern Angular uses to provide components with their dependencies (like services).

-   **`inject()` Function**: Used within a component to request an instance of a service.
    ```typescript
    housingService = inject(HousingService);
    ```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Using dependency injection leads to loosely coupled code, which has several benefits:
> - **Testability**: Dependencies can be easily mocked in unit tests.
> - **Reusability**: Services can be reused across many components.
> - **Maintainability**: You can change the internal logic of a service without affecting the components that use it.


## Using the Service in Components


1.  **Inject the Service**: Add a property to your component class to hold the injected service instance.
2.  **Call Service Methods**: Use the service instance to call its methods, typically within the `constructor` or lifecycle hooks, to fetch data.

```typescript
// In details.component.ts
export class DetailsComponent {
  route: ActivatedRoute = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  housingService = inject(HousingService);
  housingLocation: HousingLocation | undefined;

  constructor() {
    const housingLocationId = Number(this.route.snapshot.params['id']);
    this.housingLocation = this.housingService.getHousingLocationById(housingLocationId);
  }
}
```

## Handling Potentially Undefined Data


When fetching data that may not exist (e.g., a location by an invalid ID), the service method may return `undefined`.

> [!TIP]
> Use the **optional chaining operator (`?.`)** in your templates to safely access properties on an object that might be `undefined`. This prevents runtime errors.

```html
<!-- Safely accessing photo and name properties -->
<img class="listing-photo" [src]="housingLocation?.photo" alt="Exterior photo of {{housingLocation?.name}}">
<h2 class="listing-heading">{{ housingLocation?.name }}</h2>
```
